\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.11}{\ignorespaces {\color  {black} We measure rotation periods for the accelerating stars by analyzing their periodograms and fitting sinusoids to their lightcurves. The $TESS$ light curve for HIP 41277 is shown in black with the best fitting sinusoid with a period of 3.7 days overplotted in red. The orange curves represent the best-fitting period adjusted by the uncertainty measured from the width of the exponentiated periodogram peak. The right-hand panel shows the exponentiated Lomb-Scargle periodogram with a significant peak at $\sim 3.7$ days and the best fitting Gaussian.}}}{33}{figure.caption.16}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{gyro-TESS}{{1.11}{33}{\rev {We measure rotation periods for the accelerating stars by analyzing their periodograms and fitting sinusoids to their lightcurves. The $TESS$ light curve for HIP 41277 is shown in black with the best fitting sinusoid with a period of 3.7 days overplotted in red. The orange curves represent the best-fitting period adjusted by the uncertainty measured from the width of the exponentiated periodogram peak. The right-hand panel shows the exponentiated Lomb-Scargle periodogram with a significant peak at $\sim 3.7$ days and the best fitting Gaussian.}}{figure.caption.16}{}}
